6153002 #edb/ref 1061 172680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1061 172681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1061 172684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153004 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1061 172685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1061 172688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1061 172689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1061 172692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1061 172693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1064 "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1065 9069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153465 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1064 "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1065 9070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153466 #edb/ref 1067 "The Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1064 "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1065 9071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153467 #edb/ref 1067 "The Rational Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1065 9072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153468 #edb/ref 1067 "Repeating Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
